[center][size=6][b]DAORework[/b][/size]
[size=4]A free mod manager, launcher and troubleshooter for Dragon Age: Origins[/size]

[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/00-cover.png[/img]

[size=3][b]Every change can be undone.[/b] Every mod is checked. Your hand-built setup comes along without reinstalling.[/size][/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]Why DAORework[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Bring the mods you already have.[/b] Mods you installed by hand in [i]override[/i] and [i]AddIns[/i] show up as [i]Found in your game[/i]. Press [b]Manage[/b] and DAORework looks after them from then on, without reinstalling anything. Official DLC is never touched.
[*][b]See what a change does before it happens.[/b] Turning a mod on or off opens a short preview: what it copies, replaces or removes, and any file another mod also changes. Nothing is written until you press [b]Apply[/b].
[*][b]Undo anything.[/b] A backup is taken before every change. [b]History[/b] lists every change DAORework made to your mods and your game folder, and [b]Undo to here[/b] puts it back exactly as it was.
[*][b]Hair, head and colour mods work together.[/b] The game reads only one [i]chargenmorphcfg.xml[/i], so normally only one such mod shows in the character creator. DAORework combines them into one file, so every mod's hairs, heads, beards and colours show.
[*][b]Know what a shared file means.[/b] When two mods ship the same file, DAORework says whose copy the game uses and what that means: one texture wins, a data table's changes are lost (look for a compatibility patch), a script's behaviour is lost. [b]Arrange[/b] picks the winner.
[*][b]Every mod is checked.[/b] Programs inside a mod are named and never installed. Your own antivirus (Microsoft Defender or whichever you use) scans every mod before it is kept, and a mod it flags is refused. A download that differs from the known official one is pointed out.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]A quick tour[/b][/size]

[b]Home: is the game ready to play?[/b] Your add-ins, override mods and combined files at a glance, your last change with Undo, and a few suggestions worth a look.
[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/01-home.png[/img]

[b]Mods: one list, with a switch for each.[/b] Add a .dazip, .zip, .7z or .rar with [i]Add mods[/i] or by dropping it on the window. Filter by add-ins, override mods, on, off, or found in your game.
[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/02-mods.png[/img]

[b]Found in your game: manage what you installed by hand[/b], one mod or several at once.
[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/03-manage.png[/img]

[b]A preview before every change.[/b] One line says what it does; the file list is behind Show details.
[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/04-preview.png[/img]

[b]Arrange and combined files:[/b] which mod wins a shared file, and which files were merged.
[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/05-arrange.png[/img]

[b]Fixes: DXVK, and the 4 GB patch where your copy allows it.[/b] It also notices when Steam's Verify integrity has undone one.
[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/06-fixes.png[/img]

[b]History: every change, with Undo.[/b]
[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/07-history.png[/img]

[b]Your look:[/b] four dark themes, or your own accent colour and background picture, per profile if you like.
[img]https://daorework.dilly-world.net/images/nexus/08-your-look.png[/img]

[i]Screenshots come from DAORework's automated window tests, with made-up mods. They were taken where the Windows antivirus scan isn't available, so mods say "Not scanned"; on Windows they show what your antivirus found.[/i]

[line]

[size=5][b]Everything it does[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Mods:[/b] add, turn on and off (one or many at once), manage mods found in your game, arrange the load order, combine [i]chargenmorphcfg.xml[/i], and undo any change.
[*][b]Fixes:[/b] installs and removes DXVK from a release you download, and the 4 GB (large address aware) patch on copies that allow it, such as GOG and EA App. Steam's own [i]daorigins.exe[/i] carries Steam's copy protection and Steam will not start it once patched, so DAORework leaves the Steam copy alone, and tells you how to undo it if it was patched before.
[*][b]Saves:[/b] [i]Back up now[/i] copies your saves into DAORework's own folder and keeps the newest ones. Your saves folder is only ever read.
[*][b]Checks:[/b] finds your install (Steam, GOG, EA App or a folder you pick) and your documents folder, including a OneDrive one, and checks the 4 GB patch, DirectX and Visual C++ runtimes, your settings file, your saves, your DLC, known community fixes, and mods in the install folder's own override that older guides used.
[*][b]Play:[/b] the big button starts the game. A Steam copy is started through Steam, with the overlay and playtime.
[*][b]Command line:[/b] [i]dao.exe[/i] does all of the above for scripts, if you want it.
[/list]

[b]What it does not do:[/b] it does not include or download any game files or mods, and it never sends anything over the internet. It only changes files in the game folder for the two fixes, each behind a preview and a backup.

[line]

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit. Nothing else to install (no .NET needed).
[*]Dragon Age: Origins, any edition. Tested on a real Steam Ultimate Edition install. GOG and EA App installs are detected but have not been tried on a real machine yet, so reports are very welcome.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Unzip anywhere.
[*]Run [b]DAORework.exe[/b].
[*]Choose [i]New profile[/i] at the top of the window. DAORework finds your game and documents folder, checks them, and Home tells you whether you are ready to play and what is worth a look.
[/list]

Your profiles, mod library and backups are kept in [i]%LOCALAPPDATA%\DAORework[/i].

[b]Coming from 0.3.x?[/b] The window now has six tabs (Home, Mods, Fixes, Saves, History, Settings) and plainer words: [i]Adopt[/i] is now [b]Manage[/b], [i]Activate[/i] and [i]Deactivate[/i] are [b]Turn on[/b] and [b]Turn off[/b], [i]Roll back[/i] is [b]Undo[/b], [i]Installed by hand[/i] is [b]Found in your game[/b], and the load order is [b]Arrange[/b]. Diagnostics, the support bundle and your game folders are under Settings. Your library, mods and backups carry over as they are.

[size=5][b]Uninstall[/b][/size]

Turn off the mods you turned on through DAORework (or undo back to the first change in History), turn off the fixes on the Fixes tab, then delete the folder you unzipped and [i]%LOCALAPPDATA%\DAORework[/i].

[size=5][b]Windows SmartScreen[/b][/size]

DAORework.exe and dao.exe are code-signed: right-click > Properties > Digital Signatures shows who published them. A new publisher still builds up reputation with SmartScreen, so Windows may still say it protected your PC: choose [b]More info[/b], then [b]Run anyway[/b]. You can also check that your download is the real one in two ways:
[list]
[*][b]Checksum:[/b] run [i]Get-FileHash[/i] on the zip in PowerShell and compare it with the .sha256 file on the [url=https://github.com/arastella625/DAORework/releases]GitHub release[/url], or with the checksum on the [url=https://daorework.dilly-world.net/]website[/url].
[*][b]Build attestation:[/b] with the GitHub CLI, [i]gh attestation verify <the zip> --repo arastella625/DAORework[/i] proves the zip was built by the project's own release workflow from its published source, and not changed since.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]How it is built, in my own words[/b][/size]

Hello! My name is Amber :) Right now I am working as a Junior Dev and lately I have been getting more into my own projects. This has been brewing for a while, but with BioWare's shutdown I felt the need to start dedicating more time on the project to get it released. The Dragon Age series have been a beloved one to me, like many others, and DAO especially a game I will always pivot back to. I've never beat a game more than this one - and that says a lot since I never finish games anymore lol. When I am not gaming or doing coding on the side, I like to watch TV (Outlander is my latest obsession), but I also have gotten pretty decent at crocheting. It's those damn visual patterns that seem so far away! (iykyk)

[b]The short version, cutting the crap so they say[/b]

I use an AI coding assistant (Anthropic's Claude) to write much of DAORework's code. I, however, do not [i]vibe code[/i]. That requires a level of ignorance that would be insulting to my bachelor's degree and [i]checks invisible watch[/i] about to be masters degree. Although AI can generate code, it does not suddenly instill clean code standards, proper design decisions, appropriate architecture into the code itself without someone actually guiding standards and decisions. It may be able to get something working without it; but I promise you when you open the back it's a miracle the thing got past the first Program.cs file (or the like) since it probably STARTS AT 15,000 LINES OF CODE (AKA a 'god file').

I assure this is not what is happening here. Although there may need to be fixes or normal debugging, there is unit and regression testing going on. Established engineering standards. The such. Yada yada this was supposed to be the short version.

[b]The concern is fair[/b]

Look, I get it. I just want to start there. You can't come to me with a concern that likely isn't to some degree super valid, especially with the way it probably makes you feel, and there are real climate consequences without any true regulation.

What I cannot ignore is the power that it puts back into ordinary hands. AI has infested the corporate world, and although we hope that would give people their extra 10 or 15 minutes back in their day, there are some who seem to think it is more important to fill the space with work. This is not how AI should be brought to us; these programs being built should offer ease with nothing expected in return. That's why this program will never be charged, EVER. It will always be free and I will continue to update this particular mod manager until the day I die. If someone chooses to take it and do something more, it is on them as I have at least offered a foundation for the next steps. I intend to make Dragon Age games accessible for as long as possible in the hopes it will continue to bring joy to old and newcomers alike.

The rest, with the receipts (the engineering standards, my decisions, the research on AI and programming, and the water question): [url=https://github.com/arastella625/DAORework/blob/main/docs/HOW_IT_IS_BUILT.md]How DAORework is built[/url].

